WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.334
Video thirteen. Banking: the bank
reconciliation, and your cheques.

00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:08.352
Your books show each bank entry that you made.

00:00:08.282 --> 00:00:11.633
The bank statement shows what reached the
bank, and when.

00:00:11.563 --> 00:00:15.582
In the bank reconciliation, you give each
entry its bank date.

00:00:15.512 --> 00:00:18.729
Then the balance as per bank agrees with the
statement.

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:24.312
N opens Banking: cheque printing, the cheque
register, and the bank reconciliation.

00:00:24.342 --> 00:00:29.159
R is Bank Reconciliation. Select the bank
account: HDFC Bank.

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:35.389
Hisaab lists each bank entry of the books: the
date, the cheque number, and the amount.

00:00:35.419 --> 00:00:40.635
The Bank Date column is empty. So the balance
as per bank is still ₹8,00,000.

00:00:41.919 --> 00:00:47.870
The statement shows the rent cheque on 5
April. Type 5-4 in Bank Date, and press Enter.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.917
Type the bank date of each entry on the
statement.

00:00:52.908 --> 00:00:58.527
The furniture cheque of ₹30,000 is not on the
statement yet. Its bank date stays empty.

00:00:58.908 --> 00:01:04.460
Now the balance as per bank is ₹6,24,000.
Compare it with your statement.

00:01:04.908 --> 00:01:09.191
Esc, and open it again: only the open cheque
stays in the list.

00:01:10.941 --> 00:01:19.159
Q opens the Cheque Register. HDFC Bank has one cheque book of
100 cheques: 3 issued, 2 reconciled, and 97 unused.

00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:24.339
Enter shows each cheque: its status, date,
party, amount and bank date.

00:01:25.639 --> 00:01:29.322
As per the books: ₹5,94,000.

00:01:29.252 --> 00:01:34.535
Add the cheque that is not cleared. As per the
bank: ₹6,24,000.

00:01:34.735 --> 00:01:38.351
Next: the Day Book, and the drill-down to each
voucher.

